      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "On October 17, 1862, William Quantrill raided Shawnee, burning the town in a brutal Civil War skirmish.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofshawnee.org/499/Community-History",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Between 1860 and 1862, the Pony Express operated in Kansas, with eleven stations including one in Shawnee along the route.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityofshawnee.org/499/Community-History",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Shawnee was officially laid out as a town in 1857, marking its formal establishment within Johnson County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shawnee%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Before and after the Civil War, Shawnee served as a key government road linking Fort Leavenworth to Fort Riley.",
